#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# find_root

#% text
# `find_root(f, [x, x0])` searches for a root of `f` near the starting point `x0` and gives it as `[x -> root]`.

#% text
# `find_root(lhs == rhs, [x, x0])` searches for a solution of the equation.

#% text
# `find_root(f, [x, x0, a, b])` searches inside the bracket from `a` to `b`, where `f` changes sign.

#% text
# `find_root([eq1, eq2, ...], [[x, x0], [y, y0], ...])` solves a system of equations from a starting point.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# From a single starting point, Newton's method is used with derivatives found symbolically; `[x, x0, x1]` uses the secant method from two points instead.

#% item
# A bracket `[x, x0, a, b]` keeps the search between `a` and `b`; `f(a)` and `f(b)` must have opposite signs.

#% item
# The result is a rule list, ready to be put into an expression with `/.`.

#% item
# Options:

#% subitem
# `digits: d` (or `working_precision: d`) works at `d` decimal digits and gives the root to them.

#% subitem
# `method:` chooses the iteration: `newton`, `secant` or `brent` (a bracketing method; `bisection` and the other bracketing names select it); `precision_goal: p` and `accuracy_goal: a` set the tolerance; `max_iterations: n` limits the steps.

#% subitem
# `compile: auto | true | false` controls whether the function is compiled for the search.

#% item
# A search that does not converge gives a failure tagged `not_converged` holding the last point and the size of the function there.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A root of a cubic near 2:

#% cell
find_root(x^3 - 2x - 5, [x, 2])
#% output
#= [x -> 2.0945514815423265]

#% text
# The point where cos(x) equals x:

#% cell
find_root(cos(x) == x, [x, 1])
#% output
#= [x -> 0.7390851332151607]

#% text
# A root in a bracket:

#% cell
find_root(sin(x), [x, 3, 4])
#% output
#= [x -> 3.141592653589793]

#% text
# A system of two equations:

#% cell
find_root([x^2 + y^2 == 4, x*y == 1], [[x, 1], [y, 0.5]])
#% output
#= [x -> 1.9318516525781364, y -> 0.5176380902050416]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The equation e^x = 3x has two solutions; the starting point picks one:

#% cell
find_root(exp(x) == 3x, [x, 0])
#% output
#= [x -> 0.6190612867359451]

#% cell
find_root(exp(x) == 3x, [x, 2])
#% output
#= [x -> 1.5121345516578426]

#% text
# Special functions: the first zero of the Bessel function J0.

#% cell
find_root(bessel_j(0, x), [x, 2])
#% output
#= [x -> 2.404825557695773]

#% text
# The first positive solution of tan(x) = x after zero:

#% cell
find_root(tan(x) == x, [x, 4.5])
#% output
#= [x -> 4.493409457909064]

#% text
# A complex start gives a complex root:

#% cell
find_root(x^2 + 1, [x, 1 + im])
#% output
#= [x -> 0.0 + 1.0im]

#% text
# A system, solved to 30 digits:

#% cell
find_root([x^2 + y^2 - 1, x - y], [[x, 1], [y, 0]], working_precision: 30)
#% output
#= [x -> 0.707106781186547524400844362105, y -> 0.707106781186547524400844362105]

#% text
# A start on the other side of the origin:

#% cell
find_root(x^2 - 2, [x, -1.5])
#% output
#= [x -> -1.4142135623730951]

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# A root to 40 digits:

#% cell
find_root(x^2 - 2, [x, 1], digits: 40)
#% output
#= [x -> 1.414213562373095048801688724209698078570]

#% text
# Compilation can be turned off:

#% cell
find_root(x^2 - 2, [x, 1], compile: false)
#% output
#= [x -> 1.4142135623730951]

#% text
# `working_precision:` is the same as `digits:`:

#% cell
find_root(cos(x) == x, [x, 1], working_precision: 30)
#% output
#= [x -> 0.739085133215160641655312087674]

#% text
# `method:` chooses the iteration, for two starting points:

#% cell
find_root(cos(x) - x, [x, 0, 1])
#% output
#= [x -> 0.7390851332151607]

#% cell
find_root(cos(x) - x, [x, 0, 1], method: brent)
#% output
#= [x -> 0.7390851332151606]

#% text
# `max_iterations:` too small gives a failure with the last point:

#% cell
find_root(cos(x) - x, [x, 1], max_iterations: 3)
#% output
#! failure(not_converged, find_root, [x -> 0.739085133385284], 2.7757526077643035e-5)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The real root of x^3 = x + 1 (the plastic number), as a value:

#% cell
x /. find_root(x^3 - x - 1, [x, 1])
#% output
#= 1.324717957244746

#% text
# Where the logistic growth curve 1/(1 + 9 exp(-t)) passes one half:

#% cell
find_root(1/(1 + 9*exp(-t)) == 1/2, [t, 1])
#% output
#= [t -> 2.1972245773362196]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The residual at the root is zero to the working precision:

#% cell
cos(x /. find_root(cos(x) == x, [x, 1], working_precision: 30)) - (x /. find_root(cos(x) == x, [x, 1], working_precision: 30))
#% output
#= ball(0, 1.01e-31)

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A function without real roots: the search fails and says where it stopped.

#% cell
find_root(x^2 + 1, [x, 1])
#% output
#! failure(not_converged, find_root, [x -> 0.0], 1.0)

#% text
# A function that tends to zero without reaching it sends the search to infinity:

#% cell
find_root(1/x, [x, 1])
#% output
#! failure(not_converged, find_root, [x -> 1688849860263936.0], 562949953421312.0)

#% text
# At a multiple root the function is flat and only about half of the digits are right:

#% cell
find_root((x - 1)^2, [x, 2])
#% output
#= [x -> 1.0000000074505806]

#% text
# A start point is required:

#% cell
find_root(x^2 - 2, x)
#% output
#! failure(argument, find_root, 2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `solve`, `n_integrate`, `n_dsolve`, `d`
